  "text": "**Solution 1** has the right final answer, though its reasoning is weak.\n\n**Why the other two fail.** Both assume the worst case is the path, giving β_p = 3 at p = 2. But the lollipop graph (a clique attached to a long path, with the single boundary vertex at the far tip) is slower. For p = 2, the dynamics is random Gauss–Seidel, so the error decays at rate roughly λ₁/|I|, where λ₁ is the Dirichlet gap of the walk killed at the boundary. On the lollipop, λ₁⁻¹ is about the clique-to-tip hitting time, which is Θ(n³). Each sweep costs |I| ≈ n steps, so the time is about n⁴ (up to logs). The path only gives n³. So β₂ ≥ 4, which rules out Solution 2's β = 3 and Solution 3's value of 3 at p = 2.\n\n**Consistency of Solution 1.** Its formula 2p/(p−1) gives 4 at p = 2, matching the lollipop heuristic. It equals 3 exactly at p = 3, so max(3, 2p/(p−1)) switches from the p-dependent exponent to the path's 3 at p = 3. That matches the problem's split at p < 3. Its barbell argument and its claim that the exponent is a relaxation time are not sound, so I am relying on the final formula matching this heuristic rather than on the stated derivation. I have not verified the p-dependence from first principles for p ≠ 2.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
